    <!-- Lead / Overview -->
    <p class="paper-lead">
      Representing the culmination of more than <strong>two decades of systematic exploration</strong> of human and mouse genome function, the <strong>ENCODE Project Consortium</strong> presents the definitive <strong>Encyclopedia of DNA Elements (ENCODE)</strong>. Encompassing over <strong>16,000 genome-wide experiments</strong> across <strong>1,489 distinct biosamples</strong>, ENCODE 4 establishes an exhaustive reference map across three core operational layers: (1) an expanded catalog of <strong>5.3 million DNase I hypersensitive sites (DHSs)</strong> and <strong>2.3 million candidate cis-regulatory elements (cCREs v4)</strong> decoded by base-resolution deep learning sequence models (BPNet/ChromBPNet); (2) an expanded transcriptome atlas uncovering nearly <strong>18,000 novel lncRNA genes</strong> and <strong>150,000 novel transcript isoforms</strong> with kinetic stability maps; and (3) ultra-deep <strong>3D chromatin interaction maps at motif-level (10 bp) resolution (~150 billion Intact Hi-C contacts)</strong> dissecting loop extrusion mechanics via targeted degron perturbations and predictive enhancer-to-gene wiring (<strong>ENCODE-rE2G</strong>).
    </p>

